1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,979
Lesson 2.2.

2
00:00:02,359 --> 00:00:03,900
The Langevin equation.

3
00:00:05,017 --> 00:00:06,437
We already have pure noise.

4
00:00:06,817 --> 00:00:10,358
Now let's add physics:
forces, friction and mass.

5
00:00:10,738 --> 00:00:13,354
The result is the Langevin equation, the

6
00:00:13,354 --> 00:00:16,493
equation of motion of a
particle in a heat bath.

7
00:00:16,873 --> 00:00:20,541
It is the equation obeyed by
the voltages of a noisy circuit,

8
00:00:20,541 --> 00:00:24,149
the oscillators of a thermodynamic
chip and, as we will see,

9
00:00:24,149 --> 00:00:26,976
the one algorithms use
to sample distributions.

10
00:00:28,083 --> 00:00:31,994
Imagine a marble in a bowl full
of honey that is also shaking.

11
00:00:32,374 --> 00:00:36,993
Three things act on it: the slope of
the bowl pushes it toward the bottom,

12
00:00:36,993 --> 00:00:41,050
the honey slows its motion, and
the shaking jostles it at random.

13
00:00:41,430 --> 00:00:45,373
If the honey is thin, the marble
oscillates before stopping.

14
00:00:45,753 --> 00:00:48,119
If it is very thick,
the marble simply creeps

15
00:00:48,119 --> 00:00:50,169
toward the bottom, without oscillating.

16
00:00:50,549 --> 00:00:53,013
In both cases it never sits still: the

17
00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:55,994
shaking keeps it exploring
around the minimum.

18
00:00:57,117 --> 00:01:02,820
In equations: mass times acceleration
equals the force from the potential,

19
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:08,139
minus the friction γ v, plus a random
force of strength √(2 γ k_B T).

20
00:01:08,519 --> 00:01:11,810
On the left you see
position versus time; on the

21
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:14,965
right, phase space,
position against velocity.

22
00:01:15,345 --> 00:01:18,320
The trajectory spirals
toward the minimum, but

23
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:20,842
the noise keeps it circling in a cloud.

24
00:01:21,222 --> 00:01:24,737
That cloud is the Boltzmann
distribution in phase space.

25
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:29,453
When friction dominates,
inertia becomes irrelevant:

26
00:01:29,453 --> 00:01:32,502
the velocity adjusts
instantly to the force.

27
00:01:32,882 --> 00:01:39,314
We drop the mass and get the
overdamped equation: ẋ = μ

28
00:01:39,314 --> 00:01:44,927
f(x) + √(2 μ k_B T) ξ(t),
with mobility μ = 1/γ.

29
00:01:45,307 --> 00:01:49,243
Both simulations use the same
potential; with high friction, the

30
00:01:49,243 --> 00:01:53,733
underdamped trajectory becomes
indistinguishable from the overdamped one.

31
00:01:54,113 --> 00:01:57,448
This is the equation we will
use almost always: it is the one

32
00:01:57,448 --> 00:02:00,345
for Landauer's double well
and for the linear solver.

33
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:04,034
Why does the noise have
exactly that strength?

34
00:02:04,414 --> 00:02:08,654
If it were larger, the system would
heat up; if smaller, it would cool down.

35
00:02:09,034 --> 00:02:12,365
Only with strength 2 μ
k_B T is the stationary

36
00:02:12,365 --> 00:02:15,262
distribution Boltzmann at temperature T.

37
00:02:15,642 --> 00:02:21,847
The diffusion coefficient is then
D = μ k_B T: the Einstein relation.

38
00:02:22,227 --> 00:02:26,850
Friction and noise are not independent;
the same temperature fixes both.

39
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:31,153
The simulation histogram matches e^(−βV).

40
00:02:32,283 --> 00:02:33,203
There are two clocks.

41
00:02:33,583 --> 00:02:39,207
The inertial time, m/γ, says how long
the velocity takes to forget its value.

42
00:02:39,587 --> 00:02:43,759
The relaxation time, γ/κ, with κ
the stiffness of the potential,

43
00:02:43,759 --> 00:02:47,539
says how long the position
takes to return to the minimum.

44
00:02:47,919 --> 00:02:52,478
If the first is much shorter, the
overdamped description is exact.

45
00:02:52,858 --> 00:02:55,934
For a one-micrometer sphere
in water, the inertial

46
00:02:55,934 --> 00:02:58,518
time is about two tenths of a microsecond.

47
00:02:58,898 --> 00:03:02,837
In a circuit, the role of
γ/κ is played by R C: with

48
00:03:02,837 --> 00:03:06,246
one kilohm and one
picofarad, one nanosecond.

49
00:03:07,350 --> 00:03:13,072
This equation will appear in every lab
of the first half: Landauer erasure, the

50
00:03:13,072 --> 00:03:18,359
Ornstein–Uhlenbeck linear solver,
Normal Computing's coupled oscillators.

51
00:03:18,739 --> 00:03:21,786
And it is also an algorithm:
to sample a Boltzmann

52
00:03:21,786 --> 00:03:25,199
distribution we only need the
gradient of the potential.

53
00:03:26,317 --> 00:03:30,945
In the next lesson we will write the
Fokker–Planck equation for the density.

54
00:03:31,325 --> 00:03:36,362
Then we will study the quadratic case,
the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process, and the

55
00:03:36,362 --> 00:03:40,948
fluctuation–dissipation theorem,
which justifies the Einstein relation.

56
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:45,809
Frequent mistakes: choosing the
noise without relating it to

57
00:03:45,809 --> 00:03:49,630
friction, using the overdamped
equation when inertia matters,

58
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:52,825
forgetting the factor of
two in the noise strength,

59
00:03:53,205 --> 00:03:56,328
and confusing mobility with
the diffusion coefficient.

60
00:03:57,450 --> 00:04:02,074
In summary: the Langevin equation
combines force, friction and noise.

61
00:04:02,454 --> 00:04:08,906
In the high-friction regime it
reduces to ẋ = μ f + √(2 μ k_B T) ξ.

62
00:04:09,286 --> 00:04:14,892
The Einstein relation, D = μ k_B T,
guarantees that the stationary state is the

63
00:04:14,892 --> 00:04:20,499
Boltzmann distribution, and device time
scales decide which description to use.

64
00:04:21,617 --> 00:04:26,484
To practice: write the equation for a
quadratic potential and its variance.

65
00:04:26,864 --> 00:04:30,289
Compute the inertial time for
a hundred-nanometer sphere.

66
00:04:30,669 --> 00:04:34,205
Show that a miscalibrated noise is
equivalent to another temperature.

67
00:04:34,585 --> 00:04:37,145
And compute an RC relaxation time.
